TP.register({id:"lp6-g6",lp:6,grade:"6",mode:"curriculum",checks:5,nextGrade:"7th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="The Last Lantern on Alder Street";const STORY=["Hollowell hangs forty paper lanterns along Alder Street on the first clear night of the new year, and someone has to walk the line with the pole and light them. For thirty-one winters that someone was Mr. Nadeau. This year Mr. Nadeau’s hands shake too much to hold the pole, so he had to choose.","He chose Priya, who was twelve and had asked him for the job every January since she was eight.","The pole was brass, longer than she was tall, with a small wick burning at the top end. Mr. Nadeau walked her out to the bridge and put it in her hands. “Start at this end,” he said. “Go slow. Do not skip one. If a lantern will not take the flame, stay with it.”","“Why would one not take the flame?”","“One always does not,” he said, and went to stand with the others at the far end of the street.","The first nineteen lit easily. Each paper shell went from gray to gold from the inside, the way a window does. Priya found a rhythm: lift, touch her wick to the lantern’s wick, count to two, lower the pole.","The twentieth would not light. The wick inside it was damp and the flame slid off it. Priya tried four times. Down at the far end of the street she could hear people talking, and a clock somewhere struck seven, and nineteen gold lanterns behind her looked exactly the way they were supposed to look.","She let the pole down and looked at the dark one. Twenty more waited past it.","Then she thought about the January she was nine, standing with her grandmother at the window above the bakery, counting the lanterns out loud. One of them had been dark that year. Her grandmother had counted it anyway, and then had not said anything for a while.","Priya stayed with it. She lifted the pole and held the flame against the damp wick, not touching and lifting away but holding, until the wick dried and caught. It took a long time. Somebody at the far end called up the street to ask if she was stuck.","She was not. She lit the other twenty, and then she walked back up the line to the bridge with the pole over her shoulder.","Mr. Nadeau was waiting there. “Forty,” he said. “Which one was the hard one?”","“The twentieth.”","He nodded. “Mrs. Agyeman’s window is over the twentieth. She is ninety-four and she does not come down to the street anymore.”","Priya looked up. Above the lantern line there were more stars than she usually bothered to notice, because the town’s lamps point their light down at the street and leave the sky alone. She decided she would ask for the pole again next year."].join("\n\n");const POEM_TITLE="First Night";const POEM_SRC="A poem by Odalys Feria.";const POEM=["Nobody hands you a whole street.\nThey hand you one pole\nand the first lamp,\nand the dark that is left over.","Count if you want to.\nThe count will not help you\nwith the one that will not take,\nthe one that is wet all the way through.","Stand there. Let the flame\nlean on it. Wet things dry\nslower than you want\nand faster than never.","Behind you, a street.\nAhead of you, a street.\nAbove you, nobody’s lamps at all,\nburning anyway."];const SKY_SRC="Article: “Why the Stars Went Out” by Dr. Leandra Vos, Night Sky Monitoring Project. January 2026.";const SKY=["On a clear, moonless night, a person with ordinary eyesight and no lights nearby can see about 2,500 stars at one time. From the middle of a lit town, the same person on the same night can usually count fewer than thirty. The stars did not change. The sky between the person and the stars did.","Outdoor lights do not stop at the thing they are aimed at. A lamp with no cap throws part of its light sideways and upward. Dust and tiny water droplets in the air scatter that stray light back down, and the whole sky above the town turns a dim gray-orange. Astronomers call it skyglow. Against a gray sky a faint star has nothing to stand out from, so it simply disappears, the way a pencil line disappears on gray paper.","Skyglow is one of the few kinds of pollution that stops the moment the cause stops. A shielded lamp has a cap over the top and a lens that sends the light down onto the ground where it is wanted. In Hollowell, 420 street lamps were replaced with shielded fixtures over two years. Readings taken from the same spot at the edge of town, before and after, showed the sky above the town about twice as dark, and the town’s bill for street lighting fell by about a fifth, because the light that had been going up into the sky had also been going out of the budget.","Not everyone expected that. Some residents thought the shielded lamps would leave the sidewalks dimmer. The new lamps put the same amount of light on the pavement, but because none of it shines into a walker’s eyes there is less glare, and several people reported seeing the curb more clearly than before.","The sky over a large city will not come back in one budget year. But a town is a smaller problem than a city, and the night above a town is one of the few things a town can get back by changing the shape of a lamp."];const COL_SRC="Text A: “I Was Wrong About the Lamps,” a column by Marisol Ferrante. Hollowell Register, January 2026.";const COL=["I sold light bulbs on Main Street for forty-one years, so when the town voted to change all four hundred and twenty street lamps, people asked me what I thought. I told them it was a waste of money and that Alder Street would end up dim as a closet. I want to put in writing that I was wrong.","Here is what I got wrong. I thought the number that mattered was how much light came out of the lamp. It is not. The number that matters is how much light lands on the sidewalk, and the old lamps were throwing a third of theirs into the air over the river, where nobody walks.","What changed my mind was not the engineer’s chart, which I did not read. It was walking home on the first night of the new year. I came up Alder Street under the new lamps and I could see the curb, and the crack in the curb, and then I looked up, which I had not done on that street in thirty years, and the sky had stars in it. A lot of stars. I stood in the road like a tourist.","I am not writing this to tell anyone the town got a bargain, although the lighting bill is down. I am writing it because I spent two years telling my neighbors the new lamps were a mistake, and some of them believed me, and they are owed a correction in the same paper that printed the mistake.","If you voted no, go stand on Alder Street after dark. Then go look at your own porch light and ask it where it is pointing."];const NEWS_SRC="Text B: “New Lamps Light First Lantern Walk” by Teodor Bako. Hollowell Register, January 2026.";const NEWS=["About 600 people walked Alder Street on the first clear night of the year, the first lantern walk held under the town’s new street lamps.","The town finished replacing 420 lamps in November, a two-year project paid for with a state grant and $96,000 of town money. Public works director Hannah Oyelowo said the shielded fixtures put the same amount of light on the pavement as the old ones while sending almost none of it upward.","Readings taken by the Night Sky Monitoring Project from a fixed point at the edge of town showed the sky above Hollowell about twice as dark as readings taken from the same spot three years ago. The town’s street lighting bill has fallen about 20 percent.","Not all of the response has been positive. Two residents told the Register at the November town meeting that they found the new lamps’ separate pools of light harder to walk under than the old even wash.","Priya Raghavan, 12, lit the forty paper lanterns along the street, a job held for the previous thirty-one years by Gideon Nadeau. Nadeau, who attended, declined to comment on the lamps."];const SRC1_CARD="Report: “Street Lighting and Pedestrian Visibility: A Field Study.” By Dr. Hannah Ozturk, lighting engineer, for the State Transportation Research Office, 2025.";const SRC1_TEXT="Glare, not total brightness, is the main reason a walker fails to see an obstacle at night. A shielded fixture that puts the same light on the pavement while keeping it out of the eye improved obstacle detection at 20 meters by 18 percent in our trials. Measure what reaches the ground, not what leaves the lamp.";const ANNOUNCE="Public works announcement, Hollowell town hall, first week of January. Two notes for residents about the street lamp project. First, the schedule. Over the next two years the town will replace four hundred and twenty street lamps with shielded fixtures, and the work begins on Alder Street because the lamps there are the oldest in town. Second, what the new lamps do. A shielded fixture has a cap over the top and a lens that aims the light down onto the pavement, so the street gets the same amount of light with far less glare in a walker's eyes. The town expects its street lighting bill to drop by about one fifth. Any block that wants to see a shielded lamp before the crews arrive can ask the public works office to install one sample lamp on its own street.";const paras=t=>t.split("\n\n").map(p=>`<p style="margin:0 0 .55em">${p}</p>`).join("");const passage=(text,title)=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea);font-size:1.5rem">${title}</h3><div class="passage sm">${paras(text)}</div>`;const srcPassage=(src,ps)=>`<article><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${src}</p>${ps.map(p=>`<p style="margin:0 0 .6em">${p}</p>`).join("")}</article>`;const oneText=(src,ps)=>`<div class="passage sm"><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${src}</p>${ps.map(p=>`<p style="margin:0 0 .55em">${p}</p>`).join("")}</div>`;const twoTexts=()=>`<style>body:not(.sup-big) .texts article p{font-size:1.06rem;line-height:1.5}</style><div class="texts">${srcPassage(COL_SRC,COL)}${srcPassage(NEWS_SRC,NEWS)}</div>`;const poemBlock=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 2px;color:var(--sea);font-size:1.5rem">${POEM_TITLE}</h3><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 8px">${POEM_SRC}</p><div class="passage sm">${POEM.map(st=>`<p style="margin:0 0 .8em;white-space:pre-line">${st}</p>`).join("")}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:12px;font-size:1.3rem">${items.map(x=>`<li style="margin-bottom:6px">${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const pills=items=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:14px;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap">${items.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.25rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:10px 18px;margin:14px 0 0;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px;text-align:left;font-size:1.25rem">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.22rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const card=(head,body)=>`<div style="flex:1 1 250px;border:3px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:10px 14px;background:var(--surface)"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 4px;text-align:left;font-size:1.2rem">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.45">${body}</p></div>`;const srcBox=()=>`<div class="passage sm" style="border-left:5px solid var(--gold);padding:8px 16px;background:var(--bg);border-radius:8px;margin-bottom:16px"><strong>From the Source 1 report:</strong> ${SRC1_TEXT}</div>`;const center=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big" style="margin:0 0 6px;font-size:1.5rem">${t}</p>`;const sv=(w,h,width,alt,inner)=>`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="${width}" role="img" aria-label="${alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">${inner}</svg>`;const T=(x,y,t,a,fs)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="${fs||15}" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="3.5">${t}</text>`;const shape=pts=>`<polygon points="${pts.map(p=>p.join(",")).join(" ")}" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`;const dash=(x1,y1,x2,y2)=>`<line x1="${x1}" y1="${y1}" x2="${x2}" y2="${y2}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>`;const sq=(x,y,dx,dy)=>`<path d="M${x+dx} ${y} v${dy} h${-dx}" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.8"/>`;const triPanel=()=>sv(120,112,200,"Triangle panel. The base is labeled 9 inches and a dashed height line from the top corner down to the base is labeled 6 inches.",shape([[34,86],[106,86],[62,38]])+dash(62,38,62,86)+sq(62,86,-11,-11)+T(70,104,"9 in")+T(66,80,"6 in","start"));const parPanel=()=>sv(136,112,220,"Parallelogram panel. The base is labeled 12 inches and a dashed height line is labeled 5 inches.",shape([[34,86],[106,86],[124,56],[52,56]])+dash(52,56,52,86)+sq(52,86,9,-9)+T(70,104,"12 in")+T(56,70,"5 in","start"));const trapPanel=()=>sv(118,112,200,"Trapezoid panel. The two parallel sides are labeled 10 inches and 6 inches, and a dashed height line is labeled 4 inches.",shape([[30,86],[100,86],[86,58],[44,58]])+dash(44,58,44,86)+sq(44,86,9,-9)+T(65,104,"10 in")+T(65,52,"6 in")+T(50,70,"4 in","start"));const compPanel=()=>sv(126,114,210,"Composite panel: a rectangle with a triangle on top. The rectangle is labeled 8 inches wide and 5 inches tall. The triangle above it is 8 inches wide with a dashed height of 3 inches.",shape([[34,88],[106,88],[106,43],[70,16],[34,43]])+`<line x1="34" y1="43" x2="106" y2="43" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`+dash(70,16,70,43)+sq(70,43,-9,-9)+T(70,106,"8 in")+T(38,70,"5 in","start")+T(74,39,"3 in","start"));const crateFig=()=>sv(400,166,520,"A box drawn in three dimensions. The front bottom edge is labeled 1 and one half feet, the front left edge is labeled one half foot, and the edge going back is labeled three quarters of a foot. Beside it is a small cube labeled one quarter foot on every edge.",`<polygon points="60,64 204,64 240,36 96,36" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <polygon points="204,64 240,36 240,84 204,112" fill="var(--gold)" fill-opacity=".45" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <rect x="60" y="64" width="144" height="48" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   ${T(132,134,"1 1/2 ft")}${T(54,94,"1/2 ft","end")}${T(250,54,"3/4 ft","start")}
   <polygon points="300,88 324,88 336,76 312,76" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.2"/>
   <polygon points="324,88 336,76 336,100 324,112" fill="var(--gold)" fill-opacity=".45" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.2"/>
   <rect x="300" y="88" width="24" height="24" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.2"/>
   ${T(318,134,"1/4 ft on")}${T(318,152,"every edge")}`);const STARS=[["Town square",14],["Main Street",26],["Alder Street",52],["Riverside Park",88],["Ridge Road",210]];function starChart(){const W=660,H=330,x0=112,xr=500,y0=46,y1=262,Y=v=>y1-v/240*(y1-y0);let s=`<text x="${(x0+xr)/2}" y="24" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" font-size="16" fill="var(--ink)">Stars counted by eye from five places in and around Hollowell</text>`;for(let v=0;v<=240;v+=40)s+=`<line x1="${x0}" y1="${Y(v)}" x2="${xr}" y2="${Y(v)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="${x0-8}" y="${Y(v)}" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="13" fill="var(--ink)">${v}</text>`;STARS.forEach((b,i)=>{const bx=x0+14+i*76;s+=`<rect x="${bx}" y="${Y(b[1])}" width="52" height="${y1-Y(b[1])}" fill="${b[1]>=160?"var(--gold)":"var(--sea)"}" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<text x="${bx+26}" y="${Y(b[1])-7}" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" font-size="14" fill="var(--ink)">${b[1]}</text>`;s+=`<text x="${bx+26}" y="${y1+20}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink)">${b[0]}</text>`});s+=`<line x1="${x0}" y1="${Y(160)}" x2="${xr}" y2="${Y(160)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/>`;["Below 160 stars","the Milky Way","cannot be seen"].forEach((t,i)=>{s+=`<text x="${xr+8}" y="${Y(160)-14+i*16}" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${t}</text>`});s+=`<line x1="${x0}" y1="${y1}" x2="${xr}" y2="${y1}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<text transform="translate(26,${(y0+y1)/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="14" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">Stars counted</text>`;s+=`<text x="${(x0+xr)/2}" y="${H-8}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="var(--muted)">Ridge Road is six miles outside town and has no street lamps</text>`;return sv(W,H,W,"Bar graph of stars counted by eye from five places: Town square 14, Main Street 26, Alder Street 52, Riverside Park 88, and Ridge Road 210, with a dashed line at 160",s)}const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up to the camera or read it to your teacher.";const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"The Last Lantern",short:"Last Lantern",parts:[{std:["RL.6.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. For each answer, read me the exact words from the story that prove it.",desc:"citing evidence from a story for one explicit answer and two inferences",html:()=>passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE),qs:["Why did Mr. Nadeau have to choose somebody else to light the lanterns this year? Read me the words that tell you.","Priya almost skips the twentieth lantern and moves on. What stops her? The story never says it in one sentence. Which words help you figure it out?","Mr. Nadeau tells Priya about Mrs. Agyeman’s window only after she has finished. Why do you think he waits? Give at least two pieces of evidence from the story."],key:["His hands shake too much to hold the pole. Reads “This year Mr. Nadeau’s hands shake too much to hold the pole, so he had to choose.” (RL.6.1, explicit)","She remembers the January she was nine, when one lantern was dark and her grandmother “had counted it anyway, and then had not said anything for a while.” Also fine: Mr. Nadeau’s instruction “Do not skip one. If a lantern will not take the flame, stay with it.” (RL.6.1, inference)","He wanted her to stay with the hard lantern because it was the rule and the job was hers, not because somebody was watching from a window. At least two pieces, such as: he gives the rule and will not explain it, “One always does not”; he “went to stand with the others at the far end of the street” instead of watching over her; afterward he asks “Which one was the hard one?” rather than checking whether all forty were lit. (RL.6.1, inference with more than one piece of evidence)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Lantern Oil",short:"Oil",parts:[{std:["6.NS.A.1"],dur:240,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up or tell your teacher each answer. Show the division you used.",desc:"dividing fractions, a visual model, a story context, a measurement problem, and finding an error",html:()=>lead("The lantern crew measures oil by the cup and paper by the meter. Nearly every job is a question about how many of one thing fit inside another."),qs:["Each small lantern holds 1/8 of a cup of oil. How many can you fill from 3/4 of a cup? Write the division and the answer.","Divide: 5/6 ÷ 1/12. Then describe or draw a model that shows why your answer is right.","A strip of paper 4 1/2 meters long is cut into pieces 3/8 of a meter long for lantern ribs. How many pieces does the strip make?","A rectangular lantern panel covers 5/8 of a square foot. The panel is 5/6 of a foot tall. How wide is it?","Write a word problem about the lantern walk that 7/8 ÷ 1/4 would solve. Then find the quotient.","Find the error. A crew member says 2/3 ÷ 4/5 = 8/15 because you multiply across. What did she do wrong? What is the correct quotient?"],key:["6 lanterns. 3/4 ÷ 1/8 = 3/4 × 8/1 = 24/4 = 6, because six 1/8-cup lanterns fit inside 3/4 of a cup. Or: common denominators, 6/8 ÷ 1/8 = 6 ÷ 1 = 6. (6.NS.A.1, quotient of fractions)","10. 5/6 ÷ 1/12 = 5/6 × 12/1 = 60/6 = 10. Model: 5/6 is the same as 10/12, so ten 1/12 pieces fit inside it. Or: common denominators, 10/12 ÷ 1/12 = 10 ÷ 1 = 10. Partial: the right answer with no model is approaching. (6.NS.A.1, visual fraction model)","12 pieces. 4 1/2 ÷ 3/8 = 9/2 × 8/3 = 72/6 = 12. Or: 3/8 of a meter is 8 pieces to 3 meters, so 4 1/2 meters gives 12. Partial: the reciprocal set up right but miscomputed is approaching. (6.NS.A.1, measurement problem)","3/4 of a foot. 5/8 ÷ 5/6 = 5/8 × 6/5 = 30/40 = 3/4. Check: 3/4 × 5/6 = 15/24 = 5/8. Or: common denominators, 15/24 ÷ 20/24 = 15 ÷ 20 = 3/4. (6.NS.A.1, area divided by a length)","Quotient 3 1/2. Any sensible story about filling or sharing, such as: a can holds 7/8 of a cup of oil and each lantern takes 1/4 of a cup, so it fills 3 1/2 lanterns. 7/8 ÷ 1/4 = 7/8 × 4/1 = 28/8 = 3 1/2. Partial: a matching story with the wrong quotient is approaching, and so is the right quotient with a story that does not match. (6.NS.A.1, story context for a division of fractions)","She multiplied the two fractions instead of multiplying by the reciprocal of the second one. Correct: 2/3 ÷ 4/5 = 2/3 × 5/4 = 10/12 = 5/6. Partial: the right quotient with no account of her error is approaching. (6.NS.A.1)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Why the Stars Went Out",short:"Dark Sky",parts:[{std:["RI.6.1"],dur:130,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. For each answer, read me the exact words from the article that prove it.",desc:"citing evidence from an article for one explicit answer and two inferences",html:()=>oneText(SKY_SRC,SKY),qs:["About how many stars can a person see on a clear, moonless night with no lights nearby? Read me the words that tell you.","The author compares a faint star to a pencil line on gray paper. What idea is that comparison supposed to make clear? Which words support your answer?","Does the author think a town’s skyglow problem can be fixed? Give at least two pieces of evidence."],key:["About 2,500. Reads “a person with ordinary eyesight and no lights nearby can see about 2,500 stars at one time.” (RI.6.1, explicit)","That a faint star needs a dark background to be seen, so the star did not get dimmer, the sky behind it got brighter. Supporting words: “The stars did not change. The sky between the person and the stars did,” and “Against a gray sky a faint star has nothing to stand out from.” (RI.6.1, inference)","Yes. At least two pieces, such as: “Skyglow is one of the few kinds of pollution that stops the moment the cause stops”; the Hollowell readings showing the sky “about twice as dark” after 420 lamps were replaced, with the lighting bill down about a fifth; and the last line, “the night above a town is one of the few things a town can get back by changing the shape of a lamp.” (RI.6.1, inference with more than one piece of evidence)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Panel Shapes",short:"Panels",parts:[{std:["6.G.A.1"],dur:195,dir:"Use the four panel drawings. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer. Include the units.",desc:"area of a triangle, a parallelogram, a trapezoid, and a composite figure, plus a missing height and an error",html:()=>lead("A paper lantern is built from flat panels. The crew has to know the area of each panel to order paper."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-wrap:wrap;gap:18px">${center(triPanel(),"Panel A")}${center(parPanel(),"Panel B")}${center(trapPanel(),"Panel C")}${center(compPanel(),"Panel D")}</div>`,qs:["Find the area of Panel A, the triangle.","Find the area of Panel B, the parallelogram.","Find the area of Panel C, the trapezoid. Tell me how you broke it up or which formula you used.","Find the area of Panel D. It is made of two shapes.","A different triangular panel has an area of 42 square inches and a base of 14 inches. How tall is it?","Find the error. A crew member says a triangle with a base of 10 inches and a height of 7 inches has an area of 70 square inches. What did the crew member forget? What is the correct area?"],key:["27 square inches. Area = 1/2 × 9 × 6 = 27. Or: 9 × 6 = 54, then halve. Partial: 54 square inches is the whole rectangle and does not count. (6.G.A.1, area of a triangle)","60 square inches. Area = base × height = 12 × 5 = 60. Watch for using the slanted side instead of the dashed height. Or: cut the triangle off one end and slide it to the other, which makes a 12 by 5 rectangle. (6.G.A.1, area of a parallelogram)","32 square inches. Using the trapezoid formula: (10 + 6) ÷ 2 × 4 = 8 × 4 = 32. Also correct by decomposing: a 6 by 4 rectangle is 24, and the two leftover triangles together make a triangle with base 4 and height 4, which is 8, and 24 + 8 = 32. (6.G.A.1, area of a trapezoid by formula or by decomposing)","52 square inches. Rectangle: 8 × 5 = 40. Triangle on top: 1/2 × 8 × 3 = 12. Total 40 + 12 = 52. Or: enclose it in an 8 by 8 rectangle, 64, and take off the two corner triangles of 6 each. Partial: 40 for the rectangle alone, or 12 for the top alone, is approaching. (6.G.A.1, composing and decomposing)","6 inches. 42 = 1/2 × 14 × h, so 42 = 7h and h = 6. Or: double the area to 84 for the whole rectangle, then 84 ÷ 14 = 6. Partial: 3 inches comes from halving once too often and does not count. (6.G.A.1, finding a missing dimension from the area)","The crew member forgot to take half. 10 × 7 = 70 is the area of a rectangle with those sides, and a triangle is half of it, so the area is 35 square inches. Partial: the right area, 35, with no account of what was forgotten is approaching. (6.G.A.1)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Two Accounts of One Night",short:"2 Accounts",parts:[{title:"One Writer’s Point of View",std:["RI.6.6"],dur:125,dir:"Read Text A to yourself. Then answer out loud, using words from the column.",desc:"determining an author’s purpose and point of view and explaining how the writing conveys it",html:()=>oneText(COL_SRC,COL),qs:["What is Marisol Ferrante’s purpose in writing this column? Which words show it most clearly?","How can you tell this is one person’s point of view and not a news report? Give two features of the writing that show it.","Ferrante says the engineer’s chart did not change her mind. Why would she put that in a column meant to persuade people? What does it tell you about how she wants to reach her readers?"],key:["To correct herself in public, because she had spent two years persuading her neighbors that the new lamps were a mistake. Clearest words: “I want to put in writing that I was wrong,” and “they are owed a correction in the same paper that printed the mistake.” (RI.6.6, author’s purpose)","Any two of these: it is written in the first person, “I sold light bulbs on Main Street”; it states judgments, “a waste of money,” “dim as a closet”; its evidence is her own experience of walking home rather than data or quoted officials; it speaks directly to the reader, “If you voted no, go stand on Alder Street after dark.” (RI.6.6, how point of view is conveyed)","She is writing to readers like herself, who would not read the chart either, so she tells them the proof is something they can go and look at. Admitting she skipped the technical evidence also makes her sound honest rather than expert, which is the only authority she is claiming. Her closing line asks the reader to go and see, not to trust a number. (RI.6.6, how the author’s point of view shapes the way the text is built)"]},{title:"Column and Report",std:["RI.6.9"],dur:130,dir:"Read both texts to yourself. They are about the same night. Then answer out loud, using details from both.",desc:"comparing how two authors present the same event",html:twoTexts,qs:["Name two facts that appear in both texts.","Name one thing Text B tells you that Text A leaves out. Then name one thing Text A tells you that Text B leaves out.","Each writer put something different at the center. What is at the center for Ferrante? What is at the center for Bako? How does that change what a reader thinks the night was about?"],key:["Any two of these shared facts: 420 street lamps were replaced, Text A “all four hundred and twenty street lamps” and Text B “420 lamps”; the new lamps put light on the pavement instead of into the air; and the town’s lighting bill is down, Text A “the lighting bill is down” and Text B “about 20 percent.” (RI.6.9, alike)","Only in Text B: about 600 walkers, the state grant and the $96,000, the public works director’s name and statement, the sky reading about twice as dark, the two residents who objected, Priya Raghavan and Gideon Nadeau. Only in Text A: that the writer had campaigned against the lamps for two years and changed her mind, that she could see the crack in the curb, that she had not looked up on that street in thirty years, and her challenge about the reader’s own porch light. (RI.6.9, contrast)","Ferrante puts her own change of mind at the center, so the night reads as proof a person can test with their own eyes and as a public apology she owes her neighbors. Bako puts the project and its measured results at the center, so the same night reads as the opening of a finished town project, with costs, readings, and both sides recorded. A reader of Text A thinks the night was about being wrong and saying so, and a reader of Text B thinks it was about what the town built and what it measured. (RI.6.9, comparing two presentations of the same event)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Crate Volume",short:"Volume",parts:[{std:["6.G.A.2"],dur:195,dir:"Use the drawing for number 1. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer. Include the units.",desc:"volume of right rectangular prisms with fractional edge lengths, by packing with unit cubes and by formula",html:()=>lead("Finished lanterns ship in crates. Every crate is a right rectangular prism, and the crew measures them in fractions of a foot."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${center(crateFig(),"A lantern crate, and one of the cubes used to pack it")}</div>`,qs:["The crate in the drawing is 1 1/2 feet long, 3/4 of a foot wide, and 1/2 of a foot high. It is packed with cubes 1/4 of a foot on every edge. How many cubes fit along each edge? How many cubes in all? What is the volume of the crate?","A taller crate measures 2/3 of a meter by 3/5 of a meter by 1 1/4 meters. Find its volume.","The floor of a paper crate has an area of 7/8 of a square foot. The crate is 2 1/2 feet tall. Find its volume.","A crate’s floor is 3/4 of a foot by 2 feet, and the crate holds 1 7/8 cubic feet. How tall is it?","Find the error. A crew member says a cube 1/2 of a foot on every edge has a volume of 1 1/2 cubic feet, because 1/2 + 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 1/2. What is wrong, and what is the correct volume?"],key:["6 cubes along the length, 3 across the width, and 2 high, because 1 1/2 ÷ 1/4 = 6, 3/4 ÷ 1/4 = 3, and 1/2 ÷ 1/4 = 2. That is 6 × 3 × 2 = 36 cubes. Each cube is (1/4)(1/4)(1/4) = 1/64 of a cubic foot, so the volume is 36/64 = 9/16 of a cubic foot. Check with the formula: 3/2 × 3/4 × 1/2 = 9/16. Partial: the cube counts right with no volume is approaching, and so is the volume from the formula with no cube count. (6.G.A.2, packing with unit fraction cubes and checking with V = lwh)","1/2 of a cubic meter. 2/3 × 3/5 × 5/4 = 30/60 = 1/2. Or: cancel before multiplying, since the 3s and the 5s cancel to leave 2/4 = 1/2. (6.G.A.2, V = lwh with fractional edge lengths)","2 3/16 cubic feet. V = Bh = 7/8 × 5/2 = 35/16 = 2 3/16. Or: 0.875 × 2.5 = 2.1875 in decimals. (6.G.A.2, V = Bh)","1 1/4 feet tall. The floor area is 3/4 × 2 = 3/2 square feet, and 15/8 ÷ 3/2 = 15/8 × 2/3 = 30/24 = 5/4. Check: 3/2 × 5/4 = 15/8 = 1 7/8. Or: 1.875 ÷ 1.5 = 1.25 in decimals. (6.G.A.2, finding a missing edge length)","Volume is found by multiplying the three edge lengths, not adding them. 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/8, so the volume is 1/8 of a cubic foot. Adding gives the total of three edges, which is not a volume at all. Partial: the right volume, 1/8, with no account of what was wrong is approaching. (6.G.A.2)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Night Watch Log",short:"Grammar",parts:[{std:["L.6.1"],dur:130,dir:"Answer out loud. When you fix a sentence, say the whole sentence the correct way.",desc:"pronoun case, intensive pronouns, shifts in person, vague pronouns, and a variation from standard English",html:()=>lead("These sentences come from the lantern crew’s notebook. Some of them need fixing."),qs:["Which one is correct, and why? “Mr. Nadeau gave the long pole to Priya and I.” Or “Mr. Nadeau gave the long pole to Priya and me.”","Fix this sentence: “Her and Theo carried the ladder down the street.”","Fill the blank with the right pronoun. Then name what kind of pronoun it is and what it does: “Priya lit the last lantern ______.”","This sentence shifts from one person to another. Fix it: “If a person wants a lantern, they should sign the list at the hall, and you can pick it up on Friday.”","This pronoun is vague. Say who it might mean, then rewrite the sentence so it is clear: “Priya told Mira that her lantern was crooked.”","Say this sentence the way standard English would: “We was the first ones on the street.”"],key:["“...to Priya and me.” The pronoun is the object of the preposition to, so it takes the objective case. Test it by dropping “Priya and”: you would say “gave the pole to me,” not “to I.” (L.6.1a, pronoun case)","“She and Theo carried the ladder down the street.” The pronoun is part of the subject, so it takes the subjective case, she, not her. (L.6.1a, pronoun case)","herself. It is an intensive pronoun, and it emphasizes the noun right before it, so the sentence stresses that Priya was the one who did it. Do not accept “her,” which is not reflexive or intensive here. (L.6.1b, intensive pronouns)","Any version that keeps one person all the way through, such as: “If a person wants a lantern, that person should sign the list at the hall and can pick it up on Friday.” Or: “If people want lanterns, they should sign the list at the hall, and they can pick them up on Friday.” The original starts in the third person and shifts to you. (L.6.1c, inappropriate shift in pronoun number and person)","“Her” could mean Priya’s lantern or Mira’s lantern, so the reader cannot tell. A clear rewrite names the owner, such as: “Priya told Mira that Mira’s lantern was crooked.” Or: “Priya told Mira, ‘Your lantern is crooked.’” (L.6.1d, vague pronoun with an unclear antecedent)","“We were the first ones on the street.” The subject we takes were, not was. (L.6.1e, recognizing a variation from standard English)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Star Count",short:"Star Count",parts:[{std:["SL.6.2"],dur:95,read:ANNOUNCE,dir:"Look at the graph. Then listen while your teacher reads the town announcement out loud. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting a graph and a spoken announcement and explaining what each one adds",html:()=>lead("Volunteers stood in five places on the same clear night and counted every star they could see without a telescope. The graph shows what they counted."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${starChart()}</div>`,qs:["What does the graph show? Use the numbers and the dashed line in your answer.","What did you learn from the announcement that the graph does not show?","Put the two together. What does the graph show about the town’s problem? What does the announcement add about the plan?"],key:["The number of stars counted by eye from five places on one clear night: 14 at the town square, 26 on Main Street, 52 on Alder Street, 88 at Riverside Park, and 210 on Ridge Road. The dashed line at 160 marks the count below which the Milky Way cannot be seen, and only Ridge Road, six miles outside town with no street lamps, rises above it. The counts climb as the places get farther from the middle of town, and the y axis starts at 0, so the bar heights are in proportion. (SL.6.2, visual and quantitative)","Any one of these: the town will replace 420 street lamps over two years; the work starts on Alder Street because its lamps are the oldest; a shielded fixture has a cap and a lens that aim the light down at the pavement; the street gets the same light with far less glare; the lighting bill is expected to drop about one fifth; a block can ask for one sample lamp before the crews arrive. Do not count “there are fewer stars in town,” because the graph already shows that. (SL.6.2, oral)","The graph shows the size and the shape of the problem: the closer to the middle of town you stand, the fewer stars you can count, and nowhere inside town reaches the 160 the Milky Way needs. The announcement adds what the town intends to do about it: 420 shielded lamps over two years, starting on Alder Street, aiming the light down instead of up, with the bill expected to fall about a fifth. The graph gives the measurement and the announcement gives the plan, and neither one alone tells a resident both. (SL.6.2, explaining how each format contributes)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Powers and Formulas",short:"Powers",parts:[{title:"Lantern Racks",std:["6.EE.A.1"],dur:130,dir:"Work on your paper. Then tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"writing and evaluating numerical expressions with whole-number exponents",fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${center(F.prism(5,5,5,{unit:22}),"A lantern rack: 5 across, 5 deep, 5 high")}</div>`,qs:["Write 4 × 4 × 4 × 4 × 4 using an exponent. Then find its value.","The picture shows one lantern rack. Write an expression with an exponent for the number of lanterns on the rack, then find its value.","Find the value of (3/4)<sup>2</sup>. Is it greater than or less than 3/4? Explain how you know.","Find the value of 2 × 5<sup>2</sup> − 18.","Find the error. A crew member says 3 + 4<sup>2</sup> × 2 = 98. What did the crew member do wrong? What is the correct value?"],key:["4<sup>5</sup> = 1,024. Step by step: 4, 16, 64, 256, 1,024. Or: group the factors, 16 × 64 = 1,024. Partial: the power written right with the value wrong is approaching. (6.EE.A.1, writing a power)","5<sup>3</sup> = 125 lanterns. The rack is 5 by 5 by 5, and 5 × 5 × 5 = 125. Partial: the expression right with the value wrong is approaching. (6.EE.A.1, writing an expression with an exponent)","9/16, which is less than 3/4. 3/4 is the same as 12/16, and 9/16 is smaller. Multiplying a number less than 1 by itself makes it smaller. Partial: 9/16 with no comparison to 3/4 is approaching. (6.EE.A.1, power of a fraction)","32. Exponent first: 5<sup>2</sup> = 25. Then 2 × 25 = 50. Then 50 − 18 = 32. Partial: 82 comes from multiplying 2 × 5 before the exponent and does not count. (6.EE.A.1, order of operations)","The crew member added first and then squared: 3 + 4 = 7, and 7<sup>2</sup> = 49, and 49 × 2 = 98. The exponent comes before the addition. Correct: 4<sup>2</sup> = 16, then 16 × 2 = 32, then 3 + 32 = 35. Partial: the right value, 35, with no account of what the crew member did is approaching. (6.EE.A.1)"]},{title:"Put In the Number",std:["6.EE.A.2c"],dur:120,dir:"Work on your paper. Hold it up or tell your teacher each answer. Include units where they are asked for.",desc:"evaluating expressions and real-world formulas at given values",qs:["Find the value of 7n − 12 when n = 8.","Find the value of 5x<sup>2</sup> when x = 3.","Find the value of a<sup>2</sup> + 3b when a = 6 and b = 7.","The number of lanterns still unlit on a street is given by u = 48 − 6t, where t is the number of minutes the lamplighter has been walking. How many are still unlit after 5 minutes?","A square lantern panel has area A = s<sup>2</sup>. Find the area when s = 2 1/2 inches. Include the units."],key:["44. 7 × 8 = 56, then 56 − 12 = 44. Partial: 56 leaves the subtraction out and is approaching. (6.EE.A.2c, substitution)","45. Do the exponent first: 3<sup>2</sup> = 9, then 5 × 9 = 45. Watch for squaring 5x and getting 225. (6.EE.A.2c, order of operations with an exponent)","57. 6<sup>2</sup> = 36, then 3 × 7 = 21, then 36 + 21 = 57. Partial: one of the two terms right is approaching. (6.EE.A.2c, two variables)","18 lanterns. 6 × 5 = 30, then 48 − 30 = 18. Partial: 30 is the number lit rather than the number still unlit and is approaching. (6.EE.A.2c, formula from a real-world problem)","6 1/4 square inches, or 6.25 square inches. (2 1/2)<sup>2</sup> = 5/2 × 5/2 = 25/4. Or: 2.5 × 2.5 = 6.25 in decimals. Partial: 5 inches from doubling instead of squaring does not count. (6.EE.A.2c, formula with a fractional value)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Story and Poem",short:"Story & Poem",parts:[{std:["RL.6.9"],dur:130,dir:"You have already read the story. Now read the poem to yourself. Then answer out loud, using both texts.",desc:"comparing a story and a poem in their approaches to the same theme",html:()=>`${poemBlock()}<details class="look-back"><summary>Read the story again</summary>${passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE)}</details>`,qs:["Name a theme that the story and the poem share. Point to one place in each text that carries it.","Both texts spend time on the one lantern that will not light. How does each one present it? What can the story do with that moment that the poem does not. What does the poem do that the story does not?","Both texts end by looking up at the sky. Compare the two endings. What does each one say about the night, and how does the difference in form change the effect?"],key:["Any theme both texts actually carry, such as: a fresh start begins with one small job and not a whole street; the hard part of a job is the piece that will not cooperate; what is overhead keeps going whether anyone tends it or not. From the story, for example, “Do not skip one. If a lantern will not take the flame, stay with it,” or Priya holding the flame against the damp wick until it caught. From the poem, “Nobody hands you a whole street. / They hand you one pole,” or “The count will not help you / with the one that will not take.” Needs a theme plus one place in each text. (RL.6.9, similar themes across genres)","The story gives the moment a cause and a history: the wick is damp, Priya tries four times, she hears the crowd and the clock, and she remembers her grandmother counting a dark lantern, and then she holds the flame until it dries. The poem gives no cause, no names, and no memory, only the instruction and the image, “Stand there. Let the flame / lean on it.” A story can supply a character, a past, and a sequence of events, so the reader sees why this person does it. A poem can compress the same idea into a few lines and an image, “Wet things dry / slower than you want / and faster than never,” which states the feeling of waiting directly instead of acting it out. (RL.6.9, contrasting the approaches of two genres)","The story’s ending is specific and personal: Priya looks up, notices more stars than usual, is told why the town’s lamps make that possible, and decides to ask for the pole again next year, so the night ends as something she chooses to repeat. The poem’s ending is bare and general: “Above you, nobody’s lamps at all, / burning anyway,” with no person named and no next year, so the night ends as a fact about the sky rather than a decision about a job. The story’s form lets it close with a character’s choice, and the poem’s form lets it close on one image whose meaning the reader has to finish. (RL.6.9, comparing how each form shapes the same ending)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Source Check",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.6.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:175,dir:"You are researching this question: do shielded street lamps make a sidewalk less safe to walk on at night? Say numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Write numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"judging sources, spotting plagiarism, paraphrasing with credit, and listing source information",html:()=>`<div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:12px">${card("Source 1",SRC1_CARD)}${card("Source 2","Web page: “Don’t Let Them Dim Your Street! BrightGuard Floodlights, 40% Off This Week!” From a company that sells floodlights. No author. No date.")}${card("Source 3","Comment under a video, by user corner_lot_dan, 2014: “My uncle says any lamp with a cap on it is half a lamp.”")}</div>${srcBox()}<div style="display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${card("Student note A","Glare, not total brightness, is the main reason a walker fails to see an obstacle at night.")}${card("Student note B","Ozturk reports that walkers miss obstacles mostly because of glare rather than because a street is too dim.")}${card("Student note C","Ozturk found that a shielded fixture “improved obstacle detection at 20 meters by 18 percent.”")}</div>`,qs:["Source 1 and Source 3 disagree about whether a capped lamp gives less light. Which source would you use in a report? Give two reasons that source is more credible.","Three students took notes from the Source 1 excerpt above. Which note is plagiarism? How could that student fix it?","Paraphrase the last sentence of the excerpt, the one about measuring what reaches the ground. Use your own words, and give the author credit.","List Source 1 the way you would at the end of a report: author, title, who published it, and the year."],key:["Source 1. Two reasons, such as: it names an author who is a lighting engineer, it was published by a state research office, it is recent, and it reports measured trial results instead of a story. Source 3 is an unnamed user repeating what somebody’s uncle said in 2014, with nothing measured. For the teacher: Source 3 is also wrong on the facts, because a cap redirects light downward rather than removing half of it. (W.6.8, credibility)","Note A. It copies the report’s sentence word for word with no quotation marks and no credit. Fix: put the copied words in quotation marks and name Ozturk, or rewrite it in new words and name Ozturk. Note B is a fair paraphrase and Note C is a correct quote. (W.6.8, avoiding plagiarism)","A real paraphrase with credit, such as: Ozturk says the measurement that matters is how much light actually lands on the ground, not how much the lamp puts out. Must name the author or the report. (W.6.8, paraphrase with credit)","Hannah Ozturk, “Street Lighting and Pedestrian Visibility: A Field Study,” State Transportation Research Office, 2025. Any order is fine as long as all four pieces are there. (W.6.8, basic bibliographic information)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Field Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"narrative",label:"Story",icon:"📖",hint:"One hour on the roof at 11:40"},std:["W.6.3"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:230,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative with a clear sequence, dialogue, descriptive details, and a fitting ending",html:()=>`<p class="big">It is the first clear night of the new year, and you have talked your family into letting you be on the roof of your building from eleven o’clock to midnight with a blanket and a notebook. At 11:40 an old weather satellite is supposed to cross the sky from the west. Write the scene. It can be real or imagined. Write 6 to 9 sentences.</p>${pills(["Getting up there","The waiting","11:40","How it ends"])}${checklist(["Show who is there and where they are","Tell what happens in order","Let the people on the roof talk","Use time words and exact details the reader can see, hear, or feel","End in a way that fits the rest of the scene"])}`,key:["Sets up the situation and introduces a narrator or characters (W.6.3)","Events unfold in a natural, logical sequence (W.6.3)","Uses dialogue, pacing, or description to develop the events or the characters (W.6.3)","Uses transition words and phrases to signal time or place (W.6.3)","Uses precise words and sensory details, such as the cold of the roof or the sound of the street below (W.6.3)","Provides a conclusion that follows from the events (W.6.3)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"informative",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"How skyglow happens, and what a town can do"},std:["W.6.2"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:230,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative piece with organized facts, transitions, and topic words",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to explain how skyglow happens and what a town can do about it. Use facts from the fact box, but put them in your own sentences. Write 5 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: skyglow",["A lamp with no cap sends part of its light sideways and upward instead of down onto the ground.","Dust and tiny water droplets in the air scatter that stray light back down, so the whole sky over a town turns a dim gray-orange. That is skyglow.","A faint star needs a dark background. Against a gray sky it has nothing to stand out from, so it disappears.","A shielded lamp has a cap over the top and a lens that aims the light down onto the pavement, where it is wanted.","Hollowell replaced 420 street lamps with shielded fixtures. Readings from the same spot showed the sky about twice as dark, and the street lighting bill fell about a fifth.","Skyglow stops as soon as the stray light stops, so a town can get its night sky back without cleaning anything up."])}${checklist(["Name your topic at the start","Group the facts in an order that makes sense","Use facts and exact words, such as shielded, scatter, and skyglow","Use transitions, and end with a conclusion"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly (W.6.2)","Organizes the information in a clear structure, such as what causes skyglow and then what fixes it (W.6.2)","Develops the topic with relevant facts and details from the fact box, in the student’s own sentences (W.6.2)","Uses transitions such as first, because, as a result (W.6.2)","Uses precise topic words, such as shielded, scatter, skyglow, or fixture (W.6.2)","Keeps a formal style and ends with a concluding statement (W.6.2)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Say the Plan",short:"Say the Plan",parts:[{std:["SL.6.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:70,dir:"Plan for 30 seconds. Then talk for 45 to 60 seconds. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"presenting a claim and findings out loud in a logical order",html:()=>`<p class="big">The lighting fund has money left for one job and not both. Make a claim about which job the town should pay for. Back it up with findings from the table.</p>${F.table(["","River road lamps","Parking lot lamps"],[["<strong>Lamps replaced</strong>","60","34"],["<strong>Cost</strong>","$71,000","$70,000"],["<strong>People walking there after dark on a usual night</strong>","about 240","about 55"],["<strong>Light each lamp now sends straight up</strong>","38 percent","9 percent"],["<strong>Expected drop in the yearly electric bill</strong>","$4,100","$1,900"],["<strong>Worth knowing</strong>","The only lit route between the school and the north neighborhood","The lots are used mostly between 7 a.m. and 6 p.m."]],{caption:"Two jobs the lighting fund could pay for"})}${pills(["My claim","Finding 1","Finding 2","So..."])}`,key:["States a clear claim: pay for the river road lamps, or pay for the parking lot lamps (SL.6.4)","Sequences ideas logically: claim, findings, closing (SL.6.4)","Uses pertinent facts and details from the table, such as the river road serving about 240 walkers against 55, or its lamps sending 38 percent of their light up against 9 percent (SL.6.4)","Keeps eye contact with the camera (SL.6.4)","Speaks with adequate volume and clear pronunciation for 45 to 60 seconds (SL.6.4)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Words in the Poem",short:"Poem Words",parts:[{std:["RL.6.4"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Read the poem again. Then answer out loud.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases in a poem, including figurative and connotative meaning, and their effect on tone",html:()=>poemBlock(),qs:["The first stanza ends with “the dark that is left over.” What does <i>left over</i> suggest about the dark, and how is that different from just saying <i>the rest of the street</i>?","In the third stanza the poet writes “Let the flame / lean on it.” What does <i>lean</i> do here that <i>touch</i> would not?","What does “faster than never” mean, and what does that phrase do to the tone of the stanza?"],key:["Left over carries the connotation of a remainder nobody wanted, something handed to you after the good part is gone, like leftover food. The rest of the street would be a neutral measurement. Left over makes the dark feel like a share of work, which is the point of the whole stanza. (RL.6.4, connotative meaning)","Lean is figurative. It gives the flame weight and patience, so that lighting the wick becomes resting against it and waiting rather than a quick contact. Touch would be an instant. The whole stanza is about holding still, and lean carries that. (RL.6.4, figurative meaning and word choice)","It means the wet wick will dry eventually, even if it is slow. The pair “slower than you want / and faster than never” turns impatience into dry comfort, so the tone moves from frustration toward steadiness. (RL.6.4, impact of word choice on tone)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Lamps per Month",short:"Rates",parts:[{std:["6.RP.A.3"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"rate and percent reasoning in a real context",html:()=>lead("The town replaced 420 street lamps over 24 months, at a steady rate."),qs:["How many lamps did the crews replace per month?","At that same rate, how many lamps would be replaced in 8 months?","Before the change, the town's street lighting bill was $70,000 a year. The bill fell about 20 percent. How many dollars is that, and what is the new bill?"],key:["17.5 lamps per month. 420 ÷ 24 = 17.5. A unit rate may come out as a decimal. Or: halve twice, 420 ÷ 24 is 210 ÷ 12 is 105 ÷ 6 = 17.5. (6.RP.A.3, unit rate)","140 lamps. 17.5 × 8 = 140, or 420 × 8/24 = 140. An equivalent-ratio table works too: 24 months to 420 lamps is 8 months to 140. (6.RP.A.3)","$14,000 off, so the new bill is about $56,000. 20 percent of 70,000 is 0.20 × 70,000 = 14,000, and 70,000 - 14,000 = 56,000. Or: 10 percent is 7,000, so 20 percent is 14,000, and the new bill is 80 percent, 0.80 × 70,000. Partial: $14,000 found with no new bill given is approaching. (6.RP.A.3, percent problem)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Above and Below Zero",short:"Signed",parts:[{std:["6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6"],n:4,dur:60,dir:"Look at the number line and the grid. Answer out loud.",desc:"using positive and negative numbers to describe opposite directions, explaining what zero means in a context, and locating rational numbers on a number line and in the coordinate plane",html:()=>lead("The thermometer on the bridge reads in degrees Celsius. The crew also measures heights from the bridge deck, where the deck itself counts as 0."),fig:()=>F.row(F.numberLine({min:-4,max:4,step:1,labelEvery:1,width:620})),fig2:()=>F.row([F.coordGrid({max:5,quad4:true,points:[[-3,2,"P"],[3,-2,"Q"]],size:340}),"The crew's grid, with the bridge deck at the origin"]),qs:["At midnight the thermometer reads -6 degrees and at noon it reads +2 degrees. What does 0 mean on this thermometer? Is 0 the same as having no temperature?","The river surface is 3 meters below the bridge deck and the lantern rope hangs 4 meters above it. Write each height as a signed number, and say what the signs mean here.","Place -2.5 and 3/4 on the number line. Which of the two is farther from 0?","Point P is at (-3, 2) and point Q is at (3, -2). Name the quadrant each one is in, and say how the two points are related."],key:["0 is the freezing point of water, the dividing line between the temperatures above it and the temperatures below it. It is not no temperature at all. Positive and negative here describe opposite directions from that line. Partial: naming 0 as freezing with no account of the two directions is approaching. (6.NS.C.5, the meaning of 0 in a context)","-3 meters for the river surface and +4 meters for the rope. The sign tells the direction from the deck: minus is below, plus is above. The deck is 0 because it is the point both are measured from. Partial: both signed numbers right with no account of what the deck stands for is approaching. (6.NS.C.5, opposite directions)","-2.5 sits halfway between -3 and -2. 3/4 sits between 0 and 1, three quarters of the way to 1. -2.5 is farther from 0, because its distance from 0 is 2.5 and 3/4 is only 0.75. Or: compare distances from 0 without placing either point, 2.5 against 0.75. Partial: both points placed right with no comparison is approaching. (6.NS.C.6, rational numbers as points on a number line)","P is in the second quadrant, where the first coordinate is negative and the second is positive. Q is in the fourth quadrant. Each is the other with both signs reversed, so they are reflections of each other across both axes, or one half turn apart about the origin. Partial: both quadrants right with no account of how the points are related is approaching. (6.NS.C.6, points with negative coordinates)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Two Variables",short:"Variables",parts:[{std:["6.EE.C.9"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"writing an equation for two quantities that change together, and reading the relationship in a table",html:()=>lead("On the night of the walk the crew hangs lanterns at a steady 4 lanterns an hour.")+F.table(["Hours worked, h","Lanterns hung, L"],[["1","?"],["2","?"],["3","?"],["4","?"]],{caption:"Hanging rate"}),qs:["Write an equation that gives the number of lanterns L in terms of the hours worked h. Which one is the dependent variable, and why?","Fill in the table for h = 1, 2, 3, and 4.","A reel starts with 50 meters of cord and each lantern uses 2 meters. Write an equation for the cord c that is left after n lanterns. Then say how its graph differs from the graph of the first equation."],key:["L = 4h. L is the dependent variable, because the number of lanterns depends on how long the crew has been working. h is the independent variable. Or: L = h × 4 or 4h = L count equally. Partial: h = 4L is the reversal and does not count. (6.EE.C.9)","4, 8, 12, 16. Each extra hour adds 4 lanterns, which is the 4 in the equation. Partial: three of the four values right is approaching. (6.EE.C.9, relating the table to the equation)","c = 50 - 2n. Its graph goes down as n grows, because each lantern takes cord away, while L = 4h goes up as h grows. Both are straight lines, but one slants down and the other slants up. Partial: the equation right with no comparison of the two graphs is approaching. (6.EE.C.9, analyzing the relationship with a graph and an equation)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Read the Star Counts",short:"Star Data",parts:[{std:["6.SP.B.5"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the graph. Work on your paper if you need to. Answer out loud.",desc:"summarizing a numerical data set: the number of observations, the attribute and its units, a measure of center, and the overall pattern with any striking deviation",html:()=>lead("The Night Sky Monitoring Project counted stars by eye from five places in and around Hollowell on the same clear night."),fig:()=>F.row(starChart()),qs:["How many observations are in this data set, and what attribute was measured? In what units?","Find the median and the mean. Which one better describes a typical place in town, and why?","Describe the overall pattern and any striking deviation."],key:["5 observations, one from each place. The attribute is how many stars a person can count by eye from that spot on a clear night, measured as a plain count of stars. (6.SP.B.5a and b). Partial: the count right with no attribute or unit named is approaching.","Median 52, at Alder Street. Mean 78, because 14 + 26 + 52 + 88 + 210 = 390 and 390 ÷ 5 = 78. The median describes a typical place better here, because the single value of 210 pulls the mean up above four of the five readings. Partial: both measures right with no choice of measure, or a choice with no reason, is approaching. (6.SP.B.5c, measures of center)","The counts climb steadily as you move away from the middle of town: 14 at the town square, 26, 52, then 88 at Riverside Park. Ridge Road at 210 is the striking deviation, far above every other reading, and the note under the graph gives the reason: it is six miles outside town and has no street lamps. Partial: the climbing pattern described with the Ridge Road reading not singled out is approaching. (6.SP.B.5d, overall pattern and striking deviations)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Porch Lights Too?",short:"Argument",parts:[{std:["W.6.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:55,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an argument with a clear claim, reasons, relevant evidence, and a concluding statement",html:()=>`<p class="big">The town has finished shielding its 420 street lamps. Now some residents want the town to require that porch lights and sign lights on private property be shielded as well. Others say the town should not tell people what to put on their own houses. Write an argument for what the town should do. Write 5 to 7 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Open with a clear claim","Give reasons, and back each one with evidence","Use evidence from the article, the column, or the news story in this game","Use words that link your claim to your reasons: because, since, for this reason","End with a concluding statement that follows from your argument"])}`,key:["Introduces a clear claim about what the town should do (W.6.1)","Supports the claim with reasons that are organized in a sensible order (W.6.1)","Uses relevant evidence, such as the readings showing the sky about twice as dark after shielding, the lighting bill falling about a fifth, Ferrante seeing the curb and the crack in the curb more clearly, or the two residents who found the separate pools of light harder to walk under (W.6.1)","Uses words and phrases that clarify the relationship between the claim and the reasons, such as because, since, for this reason (W.6.1)","Provides a concluding statement that follows from the argument (W.6.1)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Growing From One Cell",short:"Growth",parts:[{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how cells build an organism and how body structures are organized",html:()=>`<p class="big">A seed planted at the start of the year becomes a plant with roots, a stem and leaves. All of it comes from cells dividing, and the new cells are not all alike.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["A plant grows larger. Explain what is happening at the level of its cells.","Put these in order from smallest to largest: organ, cell, tissue, organism. Then give one example of each from a plant.","All these cells came from one cell, and yet a root cell is not the same as a leaf cell. What does that tell you about cells in a growing organism?"],key:["Cells divide to make more cells, and the new cells take in material and grow. Growth is an increase in the number and the size of cells, not the stretching of existing ones. Partial: saying the cells get bigger, with nothing said about cells dividing to make more of them, is approaching. (LS1)","Cell, tissue, organ, organism. For example a leaf cell, the tissue inside a leaf, the leaf itself as an organ, and the whole plant as the organism. Partial: the four in the right order with an example missing or wrong for one of them is approaching. (LS1)","They specialize: cells with the same instructions develop into different types that do different jobs, and structures are built from groups of cells doing one job together. Partial: saying the cells are different because they are in different places, with nothing said about cells specializing to do different jobs, is approaching. (LS1)"]},{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting data on what a growing plant needs and tracing matter into new tissue",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four seedlings were started on the same day and measured after three weeks.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Seedling</th><th>Light</th><th>Water</th><th>Height</th><th>Color</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>full</td><td>yes</td><td>19 cm</td><td>deep green</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>none</td><td>yes</td><td>23 cm</td><td>pale yellow</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>full</td><td>none after week 1</td><td>6 cm</td><td>green, wilted</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>none</td><td>none after week 1</td><td>5 cm</td><td>pale, collapsed</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Seedling 2 grew taller than seedling 1 and is pale yellow. Explain both results.","Seedling 2 was taller. Was it healthier? Explain how you decide.","Where did the material in seedling 1’s new stem and leaves come from?"],key:["Without light it stretched upward searching for light and it could not make the green pigment or run photosynthesis, so it is tall, weak and pale. It grew using the food stored in the seed, which will run out. Partial: accounting for the pale color with nothing said about the stretching toward light, or the other way round, is approaching. (LS1)","No. Height alone does not show health: it had no light, could not make food, and will die once the seed’s store is used. Color, strength and the ability to make food are better measures. Partial: answering no with nothing said about what a better measure of health would be is approaching. (LS1)","Mostly from carbon dioxide taken in through the leaves and water taken up by the roots, built into sugars and then into plant tissue, with a small amount of minerals from the soil. Partial: answering that the material comes from the soil is approaching, since minerals do come from there, but most of the new tissue comes from air and water. (LS1)"]}]},{id:21,title:"What Comes Next",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"predicting and explaining the pattern of inherited traits across offspring",html:()=>`<p class="big">A gardener crossed two pea plants and recorded the flowers of their offspring. One parent had purple flowers, the other white.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cross</th><th>Offspring flowers</th></tr>
     <tr><td>purple parent x white parent</td><td>all 24 purple</td></tr>
     <tr><td>two of those offspring crossed</td><td>31 purple, 11 white</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["In the first cross every offspring was purple. What does that tell you about the two gene versions for flower color?","White flowers came back in the second cross. Explain how a trait can disappear in one generation and return in the next.","The second cross gave roughly three purple for every one white. Why is the result close to but not exactly three to one?"],key:["One version is expressed over the other: with one of each, the plant shows purple, so purple is the version that is displayed and white is the one that is hidden. Partial: saying purple is stronger, with nothing said about the plant carrying one version of each and showing only one, is approaching. (LS3)","The purple offspring each carried a hidden white version as well. When two of them were crossed, some offspring received the white version from both parents, so white was expressed again. Partial: saying the white version was still there, with nothing said about an offspring receiving it from both parents, is approaching. (LS3)","Which version each offspring receives is a matter of chance, so the ratio is a probability rather than a guarantee. Small numbers of offspring vary around it, and larger numbers come closer to it. Partial: saying the count will not come out exact, with nothing said about three to one being a probability, is approaching. (LS3)"]},{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"locating genetic information and distinguishing inherited from acquired change",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three changes are reported in a garden at the start of the season. One plant has a pale streak down every leaf, and so do all of its seedlings. One plant was cut back hard and grew bushier. One plant was staked and now grows straight.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("leaf",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("herb",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which of the three changes is inherited, and how does the description tell you?","Where is the information for an inherited trait kept, and how does a seed come to carry two sets of it?","A gardener wants every plant identical to one favorite plant. Should seeds or cuttings be used, and why?"],key:["The pale streak, because it reappeared in the seedlings, which means it is carried in the genes. Cutting back and staking changed only those individual plants during their lives. Partial: naming the pale streak with nothing said about its reappearing in the seedlings being the evidence is approaching. (LS3)","In genes on chromosomes inside the nucleus of the cell. A seed forms when a cell from each parent joins, so it receives one set of chromosomes from each. Partial: naming genes or the nucleus with nothing said about one set of chromosomes coming from each parent is approaching. (LS3)","Cuttings, because a cutting carries the same genes as the parent, so every plant is identical. Seeds carry new combinations from two parents, so the plants would vary. Partial: choosing cuttings with no reason given, or with speed given as the reason rather than identical genes, is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:22,title:"A New Year of Weather",short:"Weather",parts:[{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting weather data to explain air masses and the water cycle",html:()=>`<p class="big">Readings were taken at one town over three days as a weather system passed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Day</th><th>Temperature</th><th>Pressure</th><th>Sky</th><th>Wind</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>12 °C</td><td>falling</td><td>high thin cloud</td><td>light, from the south</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>14 °C</td><td>low</td><td>heavy cloud, rain</td><td>strong, shifting</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>4 °C</td><td>rising</td><td>clearing</td><td>brisk, from the north</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cloud",74)}${ico("rain",74)}${ico("wind",74)}</div>`,qs:["Describe what happened across these three days in terms of air masses meeting.","The pressure fell before the rain and rose after it. What use is that pattern to someone forecasting?","Where did the rain that fell on day 2 come from before it was a cloud?"],key:["Warm moist air moved in from the south, then a cold air mass arrived from the north and pushed under it, lifting the warm air so its moisture condensed and fell as rain. After the front passed, colder drier air settled in. Partial: saying a cold front came through, with nothing said about the cold air pushing under the warm air and lifting it, is approaching. (ESS2)","Falling pressure usually comes before a storm and rising pressure after it, so pressure gives warning of what is coming before the sky shows it. Patterns in measurements are what forecasts are built on. Partial: saying falling pressure means rain, with nothing said about the pattern giving warning before the sky shows it, is approaching. (ESS2)","It evaporated from the ocean and from wet land, rose as water vapor, and was carried by the wind until it cooled enough to condense into cloud droplets and fall. Partial: naming evaporation from the ocean with the condensation into cloud droplets left out is approaching. (ESS2)"]},{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how uneven heating drives air and water movement",html:()=>`<p class="big">The sun heats the ground more strongly near the equator than near the poles, and it heats land faster than water.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sun",74)}${ico("globe",74)}${ico("wave",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain how uneven heating sets air in motion, step by step.","A lake shore is breezy on a sunny afternoon, with the breeze blowing from the water toward the land. Explain why, using what you just said.","Name two ways energy from the sun gets moved from warm parts of Earth toward cold parts."],key:["Warmed air expands and becomes less dense, so it rises. Cooler denser air moves in underneath to take its place, and that movement is wind. Uneven heating makes pressure differences, and air flows between them. Partial: saying warm air rises, with nothing said about cooler denser air moving in underneath and that movement being the wind, is approaching. (ESS2)","The land warms faster than the water, so air above the land rises and cooler air from over the water flows in to replace it. That inflow is the breeze. Partial: saying the air over the land rises, with nothing said about land warming faster than water, is approaching. (ESS2)","Winds in the atmosphere and currents in the ocean. Both carry warm material toward cold regions and cold material back. Partial: naming the winds alone, with nothing said about ocean currents, is approaching. (ESS2)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Setting a Target",short:"Resources",parts:[{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing resource use data and deciding where a change would do the most good",html:()=>`<p class="big">A school recorded where its electricity went last year and set out to cut its use.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Use</th><th>Share of the total</th></tr>
     <tr><td>heating and cooling</td><td>44 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lighting</td><td>26 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>computers and screens</td><td>19 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>everything else</td><td>11 percent</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("calculator",74)}</div>`,qs:["Where should the school look first for a large saving, and what in the data supports that?","A student suggests switching off the hallway lights, which are a small part of lighting. Why might that still be worth doing even though the saving is small?","The school’s electricity comes from a mix of sources, some of them nonrenewable. Explain why using less matters even if the supply has not run out."],key:["Heating and cooling, because it is the largest share at 44 percent, so a given percentage cut there saves more than the same cut anywhere else. Partial: naming heating and cooling with the 44 percent not quoted from the data is approaching. (ESS3)","It costs nothing, it is easy to keep doing, and small savings add up across a year. It also changes habits, which can lead to larger changes. Data decides size, not whether something is worth doing. Partial: saying every bit helps, with no reason given such as the cost being nothing or small savings adding up, is approaching. (ESS3)","Because producing the electricity has effects now, including burning fuel and the waste and emissions that go with it, and the nonrenewable part cannot be replaced. Using less reduces the effects and stretches the supply. Partial: saying the supply will run out one day, with nothing said about the effects of producing the electricity now, is approaching. (ESS3)"]},{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"evaluating how a human change affects a local system and what evidence would test it",html:()=>`<p class="big">A town plans to pave a large gravel lot to make a car park. A neighbor says the creek below it will flood more often afterward.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("drop",74)}${ico("wave",74)}${ico("tractor",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain the neighbor’s reasoning, using what happens to rain on gravel and on pavement.","Name one thing the town could build or plant to reduce the effect without giving up the car park.","What measurements would you take before and after paving to find out whether the neighbor was right?"],key:["Rain soaks into gravel, so much of it enters the ground slowly. Pavement sheds almost all of it, so the same storm sends far more water to the creek far faster, which raises the peak and can overflow the banks. Partial: saying pavement makes more runoff, with nothing said about the water reaching the creek faster and raising the peak, is approaching. (ESS3)","Any workable answer: permeable paving, a holding basin or rain garden that stores runoff and releases it slowly, planted strips along the edges, or a channel that spreads the water out. Partial: naming a measure that would mean losing the car park, which the question rules out, is not yet there; a workable measure with no account of how it holds water back is approaching. (ESS3)","Rainfall amounts, how high and how fast the creek rises after a storm of a given size, and how long it stays high. Comparing storms of similar size before and after is what makes the test fair. Partial: naming the creek height with nothing said about matching storms of similar size before and after is approaching. (ESS3)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Energy Changing Form",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing energy transformations and transfers through everyday devices",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp is switched on, a kettle heats, and a wind up toy is wound and let go.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("soup",74)}${ico("soccerBall",74)}</div>`,qs:["For the lamp, name the energy going in and the two main forms coming out.","A wound up toy holds energy before it moves. What kind, and what does it turn into when released?","A kettle left to cool reaches room temperature. Has its thermal energy been destroyed? Explain."],key:["Electrical energy goes in, and light and thermal energy come out. A lamp always warms as well as lights, which is why some lamps are hot to touch. Partial: naming electrical in and light out with the thermal energy left out is approaching. (PS3)","Stored energy in the wound spring, a kind of potential energy. When released it is transferred to kinetic energy of the moving parts, with some going to sound and thermal energy from friction. Partial: naming the stored energy in the spring with nothing said about it becoming kinetic energy of the moving parts is approaching. (PS3)","No. It was transferred out of the kettle into the air and the surface it stood on, warming them slightly. Energy moves from warmer to cooler until they match; it is not used up. Partial: answering no, with nothing said about where the energy went, is approaching. (PS3)"]},{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"using data to relate kinetic energy to mass and speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">Carts were rolled into a block and the distance the block slid was measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cart</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed</th><th>Block slid</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>1 kg</td><td>1 m/s</td><td>3 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>2 kg</td><td>1 m/s</td><td>6 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>1 kg</td><td>2 m/s</td><td>12 cm</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("truck",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Compare carts 1 and 2. What does doubling the mass do to the distance the block slides?","Compare carts 1 and 3. What does doubling the speed do, and how is that different from doubling the mass?","Using the pattern in this table, predict what a 2 kg cart at 2 m/s would do, and explain your reasoning."],key:["It doubles it, from 3 cm to 6 cm, which shows kinetic energy rising in step with mass. Partial: saying the block slides further with the 3 cm and 6 cm not quoted is approaching. (PS3)","It multiplies the distance by four, from 3 cm to 12 cm, so speed has a much stronger effect than mass. Kinetic energy depends on the square of the speed but only directly on the mass. Partial: reading four times off the table with nothing said about that being a stronger effect than doubling the mass is approaching. (PS3)","About 24 cm: double the mass doubles it to 6, and double the speed multiplies by four, 6 x 4 = 24. Credit the reasoning even if the student states it as 2 x 4 times the original 3 cm. Partial: a prediction of 12 cm, which applies only one of the two changes, is approaching. (PS3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Redesign It",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. Write your criteria and constraints on your paper.",desc:"defining a design problem and identifying what makes a criterion testable",html:()=>`<p class="big">A classroom door slams in the draught and startles everyone. Available: felt, foam strip, string, a small weight, tape, and one lunch break. The door must still close fully, must open from both sides, and nothing may be screwed into the door.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("wind",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence that names the need, not a device.","Write two criteria that could be measured and two constraints from the description.","One student writes the criterion as the door should be quieter. Why is that hard to test, and how would you rewrite it?"],key:["Something like: stop the classroom door from slamming while it still closes and opens normally. Partial: a sentence that names a felt pad or a stopper rather than the result to be achieved is approaching. (ETS1)","Criteria: the door closes without a bang in a draught, it takes at least some number of seconds to close, it works on repeated tries. Constraints: only the listed materials, one lunch break, the door must close fully and open from both sides, nothing screwed in. Partial: two criteria and one constraint, or one of each, is approaching. (ETS1)","Quieter has no measure, so nobody can say whether it was met. Rewrite it with something countable, such as the door closing without a bang in ten out of ten tries, or taking more than two seconds to close. Partial: saying quieter is too vague, with no countable version offered in its place, is approaching. (ETS1)"]},{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing tested solutions and explaining why design proceeds by iteration",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three ideas were tried with the same draught, ten closings each.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Idea</th><th>Bangs in ten closings</th><th>Door closes fully?</th><th>Opens both sides?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: felt pad on the frame</td><td>1</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: thick foam strip</td><td>0</td><td>no, it springs open</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: string and weight on the handle</td><td>4</td><td>yes</td><td>stiff from one side</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("board",74)}${ico("clock",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which idea best meets the criteria and constraints? Use the numbers.","Idea B stopped every bang and is still not the answer. Explain using a constraint.","Idea A still banged once in ten. Describe the next change you would test and the result that would count as success."],key:["Idea A. One bang in ten closings, the door still closes fully, and it opens from both sides, so it meets both constraints while cutting the problem sharply. Partial: naming Idea A with no number from the table used to support it is approaching. (ETS1)","The door no longer closes fully, which was a constraint, because the thick foam pushes it back open. A solution that breaks a requirement has traded one problem for another. Partial: saying Idea B used too much foam, with nothing said about full closing being a constraint it breaks, is approaching. (ETS1)","Any testable change: add a second felt pad, use a slightly thicker felt, or move the pad to where the door strikes first, then run another ten closings. Success would be zero bangs in ten with the door still closing fully. Partial: a change named with no result stated that would count as success is approaching. (ETS1)"]}]},{id:26,title:"The First New Beginning",short:"First Start",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the shift from foraging to settled farming from the evidence a village leaves",html:()=>`<p class="big">One of the largest changes in human history has no battle and no king attached to it. People who had moved with the seasons for thousands of years began to plant, to keep animals and to stay. Dug out villages from that change show mud brick houses packed close together, grinding stones worn into hollows, ovens shared between households, and bins of stored grain inside the houses.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}</div>`,qs:["Grinding stones worn into deep hollows take years of daily use. What does that wear tell you that a single find of grain would not?","Grain stored inside houses rather than in one central store suggests what about who owned the food?","Settling in one place brought new problems that moving people did not have. Name two of them and say why staying put caused each."],key:["That the same people used the same spot day after day for a long time, so the site was a permanent home rather than a seasonal camp. Wear measures time, which a single object cannot. Partial: saying they used it a lot with nothing said about wear measuring time is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","That households held their own supply, so food and the wealth it represented were family property rather than pooled by the whole village. Partial: noting the separate stores with nothing said about family property is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","Any two with a reason: rubbish and waste pile up where people stay; disease spreads in crowded housing and from kept animals; stored food can be stolen, so it must be guarded; crop failure cannot be answered by moving on; and land has to be owned and inherited, which creates disputes. Partial: one problem named with a reason is approaching, since the question asks for two. (HSS-6.1)"]},{std:["HSS-6.2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the founding of cities, the unification of Egypt and the move of the Kushite capital",html:()=>`<p class="big">Mesopotamian cities began as villages that grew around a temple and a market, each becoming a city-state with its own ruler. Egypt’s story starts differently: Upper Egypt and Lower Egypt were brought under one ruler. After that a single government held the whole valley. Kush later moved its capital upriver from Napata to Meroe, where there was more rainfall, more ironworking fuel and better access to trade routes away from the Nile.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("flag",74)}${ico("sailboat",74)}</div>`,qs:["Mesopotamian cities grew around a temple and a market. What does it tell you that those two things sat at the center of a new city?","Egypt was unified under one ruler while Mesopotamia stayed a set of separate city-states. Name one thing a unified Egypt could do that separate city-states could not.","Kush moved its capital to a place with more rain, more fuel and better routes. What does moving a capital for those reasons tell you about what the kingdom was planning?"],key:["The two things people came for were belief and exchange, so religion and the economy were the organizing centers of urban life from the start, and whoever controlled either had influence over the city. Partial: naming the temple and the market with nothing said about who held influence is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","Direct the whole valley’s labor and water at once: large irrigation and building projects, one tax system, one defense of the whole river, and no wars between cities over water. Accept any answer that rests on scale and single command. Partial: one advantage of single command named is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","That it was planning for an economy based on its own iron production and on trade it controlled, rather than depending on the Nile route and on Egypt. A capital is placed where the future wealth is expected to come from. Partial: noting the move with nothing said about the economy it was planning for is approaching. (HSS-6.2)"]}]},{id:27,title:"New Agreements, New Governments",short:"Agreements",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Hebrew origins, the agreement at the center of the belief, and the united kingdom",html:()=>`<p class="big">Hebrew tradition begins with a family that left the city of Ur in Mesopotamia and settled in Canaan. At the center of Judaism was an agreement, held to be binding on both sides, between one God and the whole people, with the law set out in the Torah: the people would keep the law, and they would be a people set apart. Centuries later the tribes united under kings. Under David and Solomon the kingdom had a capital at Jerusalem with a temple built there.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("key",74)}</div>`,qs:["The Hebrews changed from a set of tribes led by judges to a single kingdom with a capital. Name one advantage and one cost of that change.","Judaism was built on an agreement with obligations on the people’s side. How is that different from believing in gods who simply have to be kept happy?","Building a temple in the capital put the religious center and the government center in the same city. What does that do for a king?"],key:["Advantage: one army, one tax system and one decision maker, so the people could act together and stand up to larger neighbors. Cost: taxes, forced labor and a loss of local tribal control, and everything depended on the character of one ruler. Partial: one of the advantage and the cost given is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","An agreement sets out conduct and holds both sides to terms, so belief becomes a standard for how to live and how to treat people, including a duty to follow the law. Keeping a god happy is about offerings and ritual rather than a code of conduct. Partial: describing the agreement with no contrast to offerings and ritual is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","It joins religious authority to political authority: pilgrims and offerings come to his city, the king is associated with the central place of belief, and opposing him can be made to look like opposing the law itself. Partial: naming the pilgrims with nothing said about the king's authority is approaching. (HSS-6.3)"]},{std:["HSS-6.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Greek colony founding and the reforms that widened Athenian democracy",html:()=>`<p class="big">When a Greek city-state grew too large for its farmland, it sent out citizens. They founded a colony on another coast. They went carrying fire from the home city’s hearth and keeping its customs. In Athens a series of reforms widened who could take part in government. Debts that bound poor farmers were canceled. A council chosen by lot prepared business for the assembly. Ordinary citizens served as jurors in the courts.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("torch",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["Founding a colony solved a problem at home. What was the problem, and what does that tell you about Greek geography?","Athens chose the members of its council by lot rather than by election. Name one advantage and one risk of choosing officials that way.","Reforms canceled debts that bound poor farmers and gave ordinary citizens a place in the courts. What was changing about who held power in Athens?"],key:["Too many people for the farmland available, since mountainous Greece has little good cropland, so surplus population was sent to coasts where there was land. Geography limited how large any one city-state could grow. Partial: naming the shortage of land with nothing said about sending people out is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","Advantage: no one can buy or campaign their way in, so wealthy families cannot capture the council and ordinary citizens actually serve. Risk: the person chosen may have no experience or ability for the job. Partial: one of the advantage and the risk given is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","Power was shifting away from the wealthy landholding families toward the broader body of citizens, so political standing stopped depending only on wealth and birth. Note it still excluded women, enslaved people and the foreign born. Partial: naming the shift with nothing said about who was still excluded is approaching. (HSS-6.4)"]}]},{id:28,title:"One Script, One Empire",short:"One Script",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the planning of Indus cities and the founding of the Maurya empire",html:()=>`<p class="big">Indus cities were laid out before they were built. Streets crossed at right angles. A raised walled section stood above the rest of the town. Brick was made to the same proportions in cities hundreds of miles apart. Much later, Chandragupta Maurya united many small kingdoms into one empire with a capital, a standing army, officials and a tax system. By then Hinduism and Buddhism had taken shape. Both of them began in India. Indian cities put Hindu temples and Buddhist halls on raised ground where everyone could see them.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("flag",74)}</div>`,qs:["Bricks of the same proportions turn up in Indus cities hundreds of miles apart. What does that uniformity suggest, and what does it not prove?","A raised walled section stood above the rest of an Indus town, and later Indian cities put Hindu temples on raised ground too. What kinds of activity would a society put up there, and why?","Chandragupta replaced many small kingdoms with one empire. Name one thing that becomes possible for ordinary people and one thing that becomes harder."],key:["It suggests shared standards, contact and probably shared authority or custom over a wide area. It does not prove one government ruled it all, since standards can spread by trade and imitation. Partial: claiming it proves one government ruled it all overstates the evidence and is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","Public and protected things: granaries, government buildings, religious buildings such as the Hindu temples and Buddhist halls the note mentions, water tanks, or the homes of officials. Height and a wall mean safety from floods and from people, and visible importance. Partial: naming a building with nothing said about what the height and the wall are for is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","Possible: trade and travel across a huge area under one set of rules, with roads and some protection. Harder: having any say, since a local ruler is replaced by a distant government, and taxes and service are demanded from far away. Partial: one of the possible and the harder given is approaching. (HSS-6.5)"]},{std:["HSS-6.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Qin unification, standardization and the claim a new dynasty makes",html:()=>`<p class="big">When the Qin unified China, the new government made three changes. It replaced the old noble territories with districts run by appointed officials. It declared one written script for the whole country. It set one standard for weights, measures and coins. A new dynasty also claimed that heaven approved it and that the family it replaced had forfeited that approval. The Qin followed Legalism, which held that order comes from strict law and punishment, and it suppressed rival schools of thought including Confucianism. The Han dynasty that followed made Confucianism the basis of government instead and began testing candidates for office on Confucian texts.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("coin",74)}</div>`,qs:["The Qin replaced noble territories with districts under appointed officials. Who gains power and who loses it in that change?","One script for the whole country was declared. Name one effect on government and one effect on culture.","Every new dynasty claimed heaven’s approval and said the old one had lost it. Why would a new government bother making that claim at all? And according to the note, what did the Han do differently from the Qin about which teaching the government would follow?"],key:["The central government and the ruler gain, because officials can be appointed, moved and removed. The old noble families lose, because their power no longer comes from land and birth. Partial: one of the gainers and the losers named is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","Government: one set of written orders, records and laws works everywhere, so a huge territory can be administered. Culture: people in different regions who speak differently can read the same texts, which ties the country together over centuries. Partial: one of the government and the culture effects given is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","Because power taken by force needs a reason people will accept. The claim turns a seizure into a correction, and it also makes the new ruler accountable, since the same argument can later be used against him. On the Han: the Qin followed Legalism and suppressed Confucianism, while the Han made Confucianism the basis of government and began testing candidates for office on Confucian texts. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the legitimacy half with nothing on the Han sits there. (HSS-6.6)"]},{std:["HSS-6.7"],n:3,dur:125,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Rome's change from kings to republic and from republic to empire",html:()=>`<p class="big">Rome began with kings. Then threw out its last king and built a republic in which officials were elected for fixed terms and no one held power alone for long. Centuries later the republic broke down, and Augustus took lasting control while keeping the old titles, the senate and the forms of the republic in place. Rome’s treatment of religion changed under the empire too. Christianity began in the Roman province of Judea with the teachings of Jesus of Nazareth, and for nearly three centuries Roman law outlawed the practice and persecuted Christians who would not give it up, taking their property, putting them on trial and putting some to death. In 313 the emperor Constantine made it legal. Before the end of that century it was the official religion of the empire.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("board",74)}${ico("trophy",74)}${ico("flag",74)}</div>`,qs:["The Roman Republic gave officials short fixed terms and usually two people in the same office. What were Romans trying to prevent?","Augustus kept the senate and the republic’s titles while holding the real power himself. Why would a ruler keep the old forms instead of abolishing them?","Name one thing that was lost when the republic became an empire, and one thing that was gained. Then, according to the note, name the change the emperor Constantine made about Christianity."],key:["One person holding power permanently, as the kings had. Short terms and shared offices mean an official can be checked by a colleague and must hand power back. Partial: naming the kings with nothing said about short terms or shared offices is approaching. (HSS-6.7)","Familiar forms make a huge change acceptable: Romans could tell themselves the republic continued, which lowered resistance. It also gave his authority the look of legality. Partial: saying it looked the same with nothing said about lowering resistance is approaching. (HSS-6.7)","Lost: elected government and the citizen’s real share in decisions, since power was decided at the top. Gained: an end to the fighting between rival commanders, and a long period of stable administration, trade and building across the Mediterranean. On Constantine: in 313 he made Christianity legal, after nearly three centuries in which Roman law had outlawed the practice and persecuted Christians who would not give it up. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the lost and gained halves with nothing on Constantine sits there. (HSS-6.7)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Several Pieces",std:["RL.7.1"],needs:["RL.6.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the story.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Give me three different pieces of evidence from the story that show Priya treats the lantern line as a job with rules and not just a walk down the street.</p><details class="look-back" open><summary>The story</summary>${passage(STORY,STORY_TITLE)}</details>`,key:["Three separate pieces, such as: she “had asked him for the job every January since she was eight”; she follows the routine exactly, “lift, touch her wick to the lantern’s wick, count to two, lower the pole”; she tried the damp wick “four times” before she set the pole down instead of moving on; she holds the flame on the wick until it dries even though somebody calls up the street to ask if she is stuck; and she walks “back up the line to the bridge” to report, where Mr. Nadeau is waiting. (RL.7.1, several pieces of textual evidence)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Signs and Fractions",std:["7.NS.A.3"],needs:["6.NS.A.1"],n:2,dir:"Work on your paper. Show your steps and hold it up.",html:()=>`<p class="big">These are temperature changes at the bridge thermometer, in degrees Celsius. A negative change means the temperature went down by that many degrees.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>Through one night the temperature changed −4 2/3 degrees in all. It changed the same amount every hour, −7/12 of a degree each hour. For how many hours was it falling?</li><li>The next night it changed −5 1/4 degrees over 1 3/4 hours, the same amount every hour. What was the change each hour?</li></ol>`,key:["8 hours. −4 2/3 ÷ (−7/12) = −14/3 × (−12/7) = 168/21 = 8. A negative divided by a negative is positive. (7.NS.A.3, division of rational numbers)","−3 degrees each hour. −5 1/4 ÷ 1 3/4 = −21/4 × 4/7 = −84/28 = −3. A negative divided by a positive is negative. (7.NS.A.3, division of rational numbers)"]},3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Build the Case",std:["RI.7.1"],needs:["RI.6.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the article.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Give me three different pieces of evidence from the article that show a shielded lamp does not leave a street darker for the people walking on it.</p><details class="look-back" open><summary>The article</summary>${oneText(SKY_SRC,SKY)}</details>`,key:["Three separate pieces, such as: “A shielded lamp has a cap over the top and a lens that sends the light down onto the ground where it is wanted”; “The new lamps put the same amount of light on the pavement”; “because none of it shines into a walker’s eyes there is less glare”; and “several people reported seeing the curb more clearly than before.” (RI.7.1, several pieces of textual evidence)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Surface and Volume",std:["7.G.B.6"],needs:["6.G.A.2"],n:2,dir:"Work on your paper. Show your steps and tell your teacher each answer. Include the units.",html:()=>`<p class="big">A crate for the tall lanterns is a right rectangular prism 2 feet long, 1 1/2 feet wide, and 3 feet high.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>Find its volume.</li><li>The crew covers the outside of the crate with paper, including the top and the bottom. Find the total surface area.</li></ol>`,key:["9 cubic feet. V = lwh = 2 × 1 1/2 × 3 = 2 × 3/2 × 3 = 9. (7.G.B.6, volume of a right prism)","27 square feet. The three pairs of faces are 2 by 1 1/2, which is 3 square feet each; 2 by 3, which is 6 square feet each; and 1 1/2 by 3, which is 4 1/2 square feet each. Total: 2(3) + 2(6) + 2(4.5) = 6 + 12 + 9 = 27. (7.G.B.6, surface area of a right prism)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Tidy the Expression",std:["7.EE.A.1"],needs:["6.EE.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Expand: 6(2n − 5)</li><li>Add and simplify: (4x + 7) + (3x − 2)</li><li>Factor: 12y + 30</li></ol>`,key:["12n − 30. Multiply 6 by each term: 6 × 2n = 12n and 6 × (−5) = −30. (7.EE.A.1, expanding a linear expression)","7x + 5. Combine the x terms, 4x + 3x = 7x, and the numbers, 7 − 2 = 5. (7.EE.A.1, adding linear expressions)","6(2y + 5). The greatest common factor of 12 and 30 is 6, and 12y ÷ 6 = 2y and 30 ÷ 6 = 5. Check by expanding: 6(2y + 5) = 12y + 30. (7.EE.A.1, factoring a linear expression)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Shift the Scene",std:["W.7.3"],needs:["W.6.3"],n:2,dir:"Write the two sentences on your paper and read them to your teacher.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Go back to the scene you just wrote about the roof. Add two sentences.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>Write a sentence that moves the scene to a different time or a different place. Start it with a phrase or a clause that signals the shift, such as “Twenty minutes later,” “By the time the street went quiet,” or “Back down in the stairwell.”</li><li>Write a new last sentence that looks back on the hour and tells what it meant to you.</li></ol>`,key:["The new sentence moves the scene to a new time or place and opens with a transition phrase or clause that signals the shift, not just “then.” (W.7.3, transitions that signal shifts from one time frame or setting to another)","The new ending reflects on the experience instead of only stopping the action, such as naming what the student noticed about the night or how the hour changed them. (W.7.3, conclusion that reflects on the experience)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.6.1","RL.6.4","RI.6.1","RI.6.6","RI.6.9","RL.6.9","RL.7.1","RI.7.1"];const WRITE_STD=["W.6.1","W.6.2","W.6.3","W.7.3"];const MATH_STD=["6.NS.A.1","6.RP.A.3","6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","6.EE.C.9","6.SP.B.5","7.NS.A.3","7.G.B.6","7.EE.A.1"];const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/&/g,"&amp;").replace(/</g,"&lt;").replace(/>/g,"&gt;").replace(/"/g,"&quot;");const dlist=rows=>`<dl style="margin:0;font-size:1.15rem;line-height:1.45">${rows.map(r=>`<dt style="font-weight:700">${r[0]}</dt><dd style="margin:0 0 8px 1em">${r[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=items=>`<ul style="margin:6px 0 0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.1rem;line-height:1.45">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const G_TOWN=[["lantern walk","an evening when a town hangs paper lanterns along a street and people walk it"],["wick","the cord in a lamp or candle that carries the flame"],["pole","here, a long brass rod with a small flame at the top, used to light a lantern overhead"],["lamplighter","the person who walks a street lighting the lamps"],["brass","a hard yellow metal"]];const G_SKY=[["skyglow","the dim glow over a town, caused by stray light scattered in the air"],["scatter","to throw or spread in many directions"],["shielded lamp","a lamp with a cap over the top so the light goes down instead of up"],["fixture","the whole lamp unit that is fixed in place on a post"],["glare","light shining straight into your eyes so that you see less, not more"],["astronomer","a scientist who studies the sky"],["reading","one measurement taken from an instrument"]];const G_PRESS=[["column","a piece in a newspaper where one named writer gives an opinion"],["point of view","the way one writer sees the subject, and what the writer wants the reader to think"],["purpose","the reason the writer wrote it"],["public works","the town office in charge of roads, lamps, and water"],["grant","money given to a town or a group to pay for a project"],["decline to comment","to say nothing when a reporter asks"]];const G_SRC=[["credible","able to be trusted"],["plagiarism","using someone else’s words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to say it in your own words"],["quote","the exact words from a source, inside quotation marks"],["obstacle","something in the way that a walker could trip on"],["pedestrian","a person walking"]];const G_POEM=[["stanza","a group of lines in a poem, set apart by a space"],["theme","the idea about people or life that a text keeps coming back to"],["genre","a kind of writing, such as a story, a poem, or a news report"]];function glossHtml(st){const s=st.std;if(s.includes("RI.6.6")||s.includes("RI.6.9"))return dlist(G_PRESS.concat(G_SKY));if(s.includes("RI.6.1")||s.includes("RI.7.1")||s.includes("SL.6.2")||s.includes("W.6.2"))return dlist(G_SKY.concat(G_TOWN));if(s.includes("W.6.8"))return dlist(G_SRC.concat(G_SKY));if(s.includes("RL.6.9"))return dlist(G_POEM.concat(G_TOWN));return dlist(G_TOWN)}const FR={"RL.6.1":["I think ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The story says, “____.” This shows that ____."],"RI.6.1":["The article says, “____.” This tells me ____.","I can tell that ____ because the article says “____” and “____.”"],"RI.6.6":["Ferrante wrote this column to ____.","I can tell it is one person’s point of view because ____.","She uses ____ instead of ____, which shows she wants readers to ____."],"RI.6.9":["Both texts tell about ____.","Text B includes ____, but Text A leaves it out.","Text A includes ____, but Text B leaves it out.","Ferrante puts ____ at the center. Bako puts ____ at the center."],"RL.6.9":["Both texts are about ____.","In the story, ____. In the poem, ____.","The story can ____, and the poem can ____.","The story ends by ____, and the poem ends by ____."],"SL.6.2":["The graph shows ____. The numbers are ____ and ____.","The announcement adds that ____.","Together they tell me ____, because ____."],"SL.6.4":["I think the town should pay for ____.","One finding is ____.","The table also shows that ____.","So the better choice is ____."],"W.6.8":["Note: ____ (Source ____)","“____” (Source ____)","I trust Source ____ more because ____.","Author: ____. Title: ____. Published by: ____. Year: ____."],"W.6.2":["Skyglow happens when ____.","First, ____.","After that, ____.","A town can ____.","In the end, ____."],"W.6.3":["The roof was ____ when ____.","“____,” ____ said.","At eleven forty, ____.","A few minutes later, ____.","In the end, ____."],"L.6.1":["The correct sentence is ____, because ____.","I would say it this way: ____.","“____” is unclear because it could mean ____ or ____."],"RL.6.4":["The word ____ suggests ____.","Saying ____ instead of ____ makes the line feel ____.","The phrase “____” changes the tone to ____."],"W.6.1":["The town should ____.","One reason is ____.","The evidence for that is ____.","For this reason, ____.","That is why ____."],"6.SP.B.5":["There are ____ observations. The attribute is ____, measured in ____.","The median is ____ and the mean is ____.","The overall pattern is ____.","The striking value is ____, because ____."],"6.EE.C.9":["The equation is ____.","____ depends on ____.","When ____ goes up by 1, ____ changes by ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const a=[];st.std.forEach(x=>(FR[x]||[]).forEach(f=>{if(!a.includes(f))a.push(f)}));return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Pronoun case</h4>`+dlist([["Subjective (does the action)","I, you, he, she, it, we, they. <em>She and Theo carried it.</em>"],["Objective (receives the action, or follows a preposition)","me, you, him, her, it, us, them. <em>He gave it to Priya and me.</em>"],["Possessive","my, your, his, her, its, our, their. <em>Her lantern was crooked.</em>"],["Quick test","Drop the other name and say it alone: “gave it to me,” not “gave it to I.”"]])+`<h4>Intensive pronouns</h4>`+dlist([["Intensive","myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, themselves, used to stress the noun just before it. <em>Priya lit it herself.</em>"]])+`<h4>Two things to watch</h4>`+dlist([["Shift in person","Do not start in the third person and switch to you. Pick one and keep it."],["Vague pronoun","If a pronoun could point at two different people, name the person instead."]])}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function multBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){const s=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-");let i=0;const num=()=>{if(s[i]==="("){i++;const v=expr();if(s[i]===")")i++;return v}if(s[i]==="-"){i++;return-num()}const m=/^(\d+\.?\d*|\.\d+)/.exec(s.slice(i));if(!m)throw new Error("n");i+=m[0].length;return+m[0]};const term=()=>{let v=num();while(s[i]==="*"||s[i]==="/"){const o=s[i++],x=num();v=o==="*"?v*x:v/x}return v};const expr=()=>{let v=term();while(s[i]==="+"||s[i]==="-"){const o=s[i++],x=term();v=o==="+"?v+x:v-x}return v};if(!s)return"";try{const v=expr();return i<s.length||!Number.isFinite(v)?"Error":String(+v.toPrecision(10))}catch(e){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","C","1","2","3","−","(","0",".","=","+",")"];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Adds, subtracts, multiplies, and divides. It has no percent, fraction, or exponent keys.</p>`}function calcBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.slice(0,-1);calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k}r()})}let fsOn={};function fsHtml(){return[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,12].map(d=>`<div class="fs-row">${Array.from({length:d},(_,i)=>`<button class="fs-part${d>=8?" sm":""}${fsOn[d+"_"+i]?" on":""}" data-fs="${d}_${i}">${d===1?"1":"1/"+d}</button>`).join("")}</div>`).join("")+`<p class="small">Click a piece to color it. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function fsBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-fs]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{fsOn[x.dataset.fs]=!fsOn[x.dataset.fs];r()})}const neg=v=>v<0?"−"+-v:String(v);let nlOn={};function nlHtml(){const marks=Object.keys(nlOn).filter(k=>nlOn[k]).map(Number);const ticks=[];for(let v=-10;v<=10;v+=2)ticks.push([v,neg(v)]);let h=`<div class="figure">${F.numberLine({min:-10,max:10,step:1,labelEvery:2,width:620,marks,hideNumbers:true,ticks})}</div><div class="nl">`;for(let i=-10;i<=10;i++)h+=`<button data-n="${i}" class="${nlOn[i]?"on":""}">${neg(i)}</button>`;return h+`</div><p class="small">Click a number to mark it on the line. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function nlBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-n]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{nlOn[x.dataset.n]=!nlOn[x.dataset.n];r()})}let rt={lab:["",""],a:["","","","",""],b:["","","","",""]};const rin=(k,i,v,w,al)=>`<input data-rt="${k}" data-i="${i}" value="${esc(v)}" aria-label="${al}" style="width:${w};max-width:100%;font:inherit;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center;padding:4px 2px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--bg);color:var(--ink)">`;function ratioHtml(){return`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:0 auto">${["a","b"].map((k,j)=>`<tr><th>${rin("lab",j,rt.lab[j],"6.5em","Name of row "+(j+1))}</th>${rt[k].map((v,i)=>`<td style="padding:4px">${rin(k,i,v,"3.2em","Row "+(j+1)+", box "+(i+1))}</td>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div><div style="text-align:center;margin-top:8px"><button class="btn ghost" data-rc="1">Clear the table</button></div><p class="small">Type a name for each row. Then fill in the numbers the student tells you.</p>`}function ratioBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-rt]").forEach(x=>x.oninput=()=>{rt[x.dataset.rt][+x.dataset.i]=x.value});body.querySelectorAll("[data-rc]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{rt={lab:["",""],a:["","","","",""],b:["","","","",""]};r()})}const FORM={"6.NS.A.1":["Dividing fractions",`<p class="formula">a/b ÷ c/d = a/b × d/c</p>`+ul(["Dividing by a fraction asks: how many of these fit inside that?","To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal, which means flipping the second fraction.","Change a mixed number to a fraction first. 4 1/2 = 9/2.","Check with multiplication. The answer times the number you divided by should give the number you started with."])],"6.G.A.1":["Area formulas",`<table class="meas"><tr><th>Rectangle</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">A = base × height</td></tr><tr><th>Parallelogram</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">A = base × height</td></tr><tr><th>Triangle</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">A = 1/2 × base × height</td></tr><tr><th>Trapezoid</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">A = (base 1 + base 2) ÷ 2 × height</td></tr></table>`+ul(["The height is always perpendicular to the base, which is what the dashed line shows.","A shape made of more than one piece: find each area and add them.","Area is in square units."])],"6.G.A.2":["Volume of a right rectangular prism",`<p class="formula">V = l × w × h, and V = B × h</p>`+ul(["B is the area of the base.","Packing with cubes of edge 1/4: divide each edge length by 1/4 to get the number of cubes along it. Each cube holds (1/4)(1/4)(1/4) = 1/64 of a cubic unit.","Volume is in cubic units.","To find a missing edge, divide the volume by the area of the base."])],"6.EE.A.1":["Exponents and order of operations",ul(["An exponent tells how many times to use the base as a factor. 2<sup>4</sup> = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16.","Order of operations: 1. parentheses, 2. exponents, 3. multiply and divide from left to right, 4. add and subtract from left to right."])],"6.EE.A.2c":["Evaluating expressions",ul(["Substitute: replace each letter with its number.","A number next to a letter means multiply. 7n means 7 × n.","5x<sup>2</sup> means 5 × x × x. Do the exponent before you multiply.","Then follow the order of operations."])],"7.NS.A.3":["Four operations with signed numbers",ul(["A negative divided by a negative is positive. A negative divided by a positive is negative.","A drop is a negative change. A rise is a positive change.","Change a mixed number to a fraction before dividing."])],"7.G.B.6":["Volume and surface area",`<p class="formula">V = l × w × h</p><p class="formula">Surface area = the sum of the areas of all the faces</p>`+ul(["A box has three pairs of matching faces, so find three areas and double each one.","Volume is in cubic units and surface area is in square units."])],"7.EE.A.1":["Linear expressions",ul(["<strong>Expand:</strong> multiply the number outside by every term inside. 6(2n − 5) = 12n − 30.","<strong>Combine like terms:</strong> add the terms with the same letter, then the plain numbers.","<strong>Factor:</strong> pull out the greatest common factor. 12y + 30 = 6(2y + 5).","Check a factoring by expanding it again."])]};function formHtml(st){return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>`<h4 style="margin:8px 0 2px">${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`).join("")}const TOOLS={gloss:{name:"Word help",std:READ_STD.concat(["SL.6.2","W.6.8","W.6.2","W.6.3","L.6.1"]),html:glossHtml},frames:{name:"Sentence frames",std:Object.keys(FR),html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.6.1"],html:gramHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:multBind},form:{name:"Formula sheet",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},frac:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.2","7.NS.A.3"],html:fsHtml,bind:fsBind},nline:{name:"Number line with negatives",std:["6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6","7.NS.A.3"],html:nlHtml,bind:nlBind},ratio:{name:"Scratch table",std:["6.RP.A.3","6.EE.C.9","6.SP.B.5","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","7.G.B.6"],html:ratioHtml,bind:ratioBind},calc:{name:"Calculator (four-function)",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"passage",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",std:["W.6.2","W.6.8","SL.6.4"],msg:"If you read the fact box, the source cards, or the table aloud, note it. The checks on this stop still stand."},{t:"gloss",std:["SL.6.2"],msg:"Word help explains skyglow, shielded lamp, glare, and reading. The student still has to read the graph and listen to the announcement."},{t:"gloss",std:["W.6.8"],msg:"Word help defines credible, plagiarism, paraphrase, and quote. This stop then shows using those ideas with a reference."},{t:"gloss",std:["RL.6.9"],msg:"Word help defines stanza, theme, and genre, so count the naming part of the theme question as supported."},{t:"frames",std:READ_STD,msg:"Sentence frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["SL.6.4","SL.6.2","W.6.8","L.6.1"]),msg:"Sentence frames supply the order, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"gram",std:["L.6.1"],msg:"The grammar card names the three pronoun cases, the intensive pronouns, and the two errors to watch for, so this stop shows using a reference rather than recall."},{t:"mult",std:["6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c"],msg:"The chart gives multiplication facts. The student still has to know what the exponent means and which step comes first."},{t:"frac",std:["6.NS.A.1"],msg:"Visual fraction models are part of this standard, so the strips do not lower the rating. Count the describe-a-model answer as supported if the strips did the showing."},{t:"frac",std:["6.G.A.2"],msg:"If the strips were used to count the quarter-foot cubes along an edge, note that the student used a model rather than dividing."},{t:"nline",std:["7.NS.A.3"],msg:"With the clickable number line, the signed-number answers show using a visual rather than working them mentally."},{t:"nline",std:["6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6"],msg:"The clickable number line shows the negative side already laid out, so count the placing of a number as supported. The explanation of what 0 means is still the student's own."},{t:"ratio",std:["6.RP.A.3"],msg:"The scratch table makes an equivalent-ratio table easy to lay out. Note whether the student decided what rows to put in it."},{t:"ratio",std:["6.SP.B.5"],msg:"If the values were listed in the scratch table before finding the median, note that the ordering step was supported."},{t:"calc",std:["6.RP.A.3","6.SP.B.5","6.EE.C.9"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate whether the student set the problem up correctly and can say what the answer means."},{t:"frames",std:["6.SP.B.5","6.EE.C.9"],msg:"The frames name each part of the summary for the student, so rate on whether the content of each part is right."},{t:"ratio",std:["6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","7.G.B.6"],msg:"The scratch table only holds the numbers the student says. It does not lower the rating, but note whether the student decided what to put in it."},{t:"form",std:["6.G.A.1"],msg:"The formula sheet gives the area formula for every shape on this stop and says the height is perpendicular to the base, so count those answers as supported."},{t:"form",std:["6.G.A.2","7.G.B.6"],msg:"The formula sheet gives V = lwh, V = Bh, and how the quarter-foot cubes work, so this stop shows applying a stated formula rather than recall."},{t:"form",std:["6.NS.A.1","6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","7.NS.A.3","7.EE.A.1"],msg:"The formula sheet gives the rules for this stop, so the stop shows applying them with a reference, not recall."},{t:"calc",std:["6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c"],msg:"This stop checks computing in the right order. With the calculator, rate only whether the student chose the right steps, and note that the arithmetic was supported."},{t:"calc",std:["6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","7.NS.A.3","7.G.B.6"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate whether the student set up each problem correctly, such as which numbers to multiply or divide."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:READ_STD.concat(["W.6.8"])}}});
